00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:50.450
- Well,

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.120
I get to start off with some
of the questions here as we wait

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.490
for them to come in
from everybody. But

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.245
I just wanted to say I loved
your Posit Comp Talk from this year.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:01.420
And

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.230
was just listening to it a
few days ago. But I love when

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:11.390
you spoke a bit about the PoC
or proof of concept that you did.

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.880
And I thought it might be
helpful to start by talking a

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:14.850
little bit about that,

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:18.515
so we can all get some context
into some of the work that you do.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.300
Would you be able to explain
a bit about the project that you

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.420
came up with to use for
your PoC and kind of get buy-in

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.010
across the company for your project?

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:30.570
- Yeah. So

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:37.350
as the first data science hire within
the org, I knew we wanted to

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.805
get information to our
business units, right? And so

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.620
what I wanted to do was
run analysis and send it to, let's

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.940
say, my manager, right?

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.600
The thing is that when I
created an R Markdown document

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.990
at the time in order to
share it with her, I had to

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.170
basically render the HTML file,

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:00.910
and send it over
because I didn't have a

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.810
server. Because it's
proprietary information,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.130
we can't share it easily.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.570
Because you don't want
it out in the world.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.670
So I'd send the
email attachment and

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.940
send it to her, and then she
would be like, Oh, this is great.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.760
But I don't want to download
something and have to open it.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.920
So I'd want be able
to click a URL link.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.790
And so in terms of what it was,
what it was it's kind of an

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:28.222
analysis of anything that she
wanted to know that's perfect for

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:30.630
her job. So let's say,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.810
like here's a meetings that
you have during the week,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.150
and then it's really insightful
information that you can't

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.960
necessarily get
from, let's say, a--

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.030
like a dashboard or
something like that.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.460
It's super specific to them.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.840
So like in terms of the idea
of the PoCs that is to kind of

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:52.730
almost give that nugget
of like, Oh, wow,

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:54.740
this is really useful to me,

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.960
and then being able to
surface that to them.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.810
And then what that does is
it gets that buy in because

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.570
oftentimes, when you're
trying to, let's say,

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:04.460
get a new service
or a new product,

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:09.370
some people will look at the
sticker price, it's like, oh, sticker shock.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:12.050
It's like, oh, wow, there's
no way I'm going to pay that.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.350
We already have Tableau. We
already have Power BI, whatever.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:18.720
Why do I want to get another
data science product I've never heard of?

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:19.700
So in order to almost

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.210
get the buy in, is you kind
of give them that, oh, wow, sure,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.610
the art of the possible
to be like, oh, you know,

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.800
here's this cool thing I was
able to do. And then they're

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.930
like, oh, my gosh, I really
like that. Tell me more.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:37.910
And then that's how you can kind
of get them to be like, oh,

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.580
this is really useful because
you kind of are surfacing

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:44.280
information that's really
relevant to them at that time.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.620
Right? So--

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:47.120
- Yeah.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:51.420
And I think that's something that's
come up quite a bit on

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:52.545
the Hangouts, too,

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:56.160
which might be good to chat
a little bit more about is the

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.625
communication piece of
your insights, but also

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.420
realizing you might need to
have some different approaches,

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.240
like not everybody is going to
go and look at a dashboard.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.140
They might need a summary
of insights in an email.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:10.350
SEAN: Yeah.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:10.455
-

00:08:10.455 --> 00:08:14.550
And I'm wondering what lessons
have you learned about that

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:15.690
communication piece.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:21.200
- Yeah. So I think the
idea of being able to proactively find

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.270
information that might
be relevant to people,

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:25.970
because like to your point,
I can create 10 dashboards,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.220
and then just be like,
oh, check the dashboards. Other

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:29.610
people, they don't have
time to do that, right?

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.370
And so sometimes
you can be like, oh,

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.000
it looks like you're
meeting with Mary

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.419
from whatever company. Did you
know that XYZ thing happened, right?

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.799
And then they can go armed,

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:42.919
ready with this knowledge
whenever they meet with this

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:45.590
individual. And they don't
have to check a dashboard.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:47.690
And so being able to find

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.220
whatever information,

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:53.300
whether it be from a
different data source, or some analysis,

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:57.350
or report, and then you're able
to send them an email, right?

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.370
Or some type
of alert dynamically, right?

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:06.140
So I use the blastula package
within R to kind of send emails

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.445
programmatically to people.

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:12.230
And so that's how I was able
to get them to really latch on to

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:13.460
that. Because to your point,

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.310
people don't want to
necessarily have to check

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:19.850
something, right?

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:23.060
It'd be nice if it was
delivered to you in your inbox.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.470
And you can be like, oh,
wow, this is really helpful.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:26.480
And then you go on your way.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:29.420
- [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:33.230
shared into the chat the
example that you referenced in

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:35.110
your talk.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:39.845
But Kelly O'Brien at
Posit built this example,

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.885
where she had like a
cookie cutter, Etsy shop.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:50.030
And she showed how she
used blastula and Posit Connect to

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.460
send out these conditional
emails. It's just a fun

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:55.280
example. I wanted to
share in the chat, too.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:55.780
SEAN: Oh, yeah. It's great.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.970
- And I'm seeing
a lot of love for

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.550
blastula here [CHUCKLES]

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:05.730
in the chat as well. So
feel free to share some--

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:07.230
oh, I think you already are--

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.095
share some resources on
that, too. Thanks, Marcos.

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:15.810
George, I see you asked
a question here in the chat.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:16.760
Do you want to jump in?

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.590
I know it's a question kind
of for the chat as well.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.040
But I thought it would be
good to discuss live, too.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.170
GEORGE: Sure, yeah.
Thanks, Rachael.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.600
Yeah, that got me
thinking about just prior

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:31.500
roles that I had.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.510
And I was wondering if anyone
else had a prior job in sales.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:40.260
And I think the way you put
it, Sean, where you said

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:44.010
the person might ask, you
know, I already have this.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.220
I already have that. Why
do I need this new thing?

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.160
I'm like, that's exactly

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.810
like the kind of
sales question and

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:56.070
interaction that I used
to have in a prior role.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.480
But I never really
thought about bringing those skills

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.070
here because I was never really
good at it. It's not like my

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.430
natural state to
try and sell things.

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:09.360
So just wondering if there's
any best practices that maybe

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.050
people have in general
from being in a sales role.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:15.930
Maybe you sold cell
phones, maybe you sold

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:18.685
cars, I don't know.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:21.580
But it seems like it's very
important in what we do.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.440
- Yeah. I mean I don't
have experience in sales.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:26.630
But I think to your point,

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:29.855
I think it's about
trying to find that

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:32.800
the way that you
can provide value.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.630
I feel like I'm
reiterating my talk, sorry.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:39.610
So to find value that the
person would be interested in, right?

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.160
And so then you're trying to
make their life a little bit

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.500
easier, whether it be
you're selling something,

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:47.410
or you're trying to give them

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:49.480
information--

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:50.140
in my case,

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:54.070
information that allows them to act
and do their job a little

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:56.770
bit better without
friction, right?

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:57.840
And so at least

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.025
for me, it was just building

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:03.550
different reports

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:06.080
that allows them to

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:11.080
do whatever they need.
One thing I did recently--

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.120
just I think on Tuesday--

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:13.620
so

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.480
I decided last year to

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.420
make a Quarto Revealjs

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:22.150
document, so a
slide deck of like--

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:23.050
we call it [? S2G ?]

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:24.790
wrapped, like the
Spotify Wrapped,

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:26.980
where I summarize all the
things that we've done

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:30.110
in the organization.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:32.420
And it's more than just
a PowerPoint deck because it

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:35.600
actually embeds all
the different widgets and

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:39.020
dashboards. So I use GT with the

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:42.130
interactivity with it. I use

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.330
all these different charts from
high charter, and all that.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.910
And it allows people to
interact with stuff and kind of

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.250
see everything as a summary.

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:55.280
And it's one of those things
that I got so much good

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:56.705
feedback, like, oh, my
gosh. This is amazing.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.140
And then allows

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:03.035
the folks to see all the
work that we've done.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.620
And so that's one thing
that I kind of created.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.260
And I would encourage
other people to do that,

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:10.355
is to use like Quarto.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:15.575
You can create an HTML file,
a PDF, or you use the Revealjs

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.120
presentation, or you can render
it as a PowerPoint as well.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:24.170
But it allows you to go from
just routine analysis to

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:27.920
something that's really amazing that
you can share with people

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:28.940
in your community.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.810
RACHAEL: I love that
idea, George, about using

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:36.970
that prior experience that
people may have learned in

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:39.025
sales. And it was
just making me think--

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:42.340
I mean, I was in
sales at Posit before.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.295
But one of my first jobs
was actually working--

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:47.800
a beer cart on the golf course,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.250
and driving around
in the golf cart. And

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.550
that in a way was
also a sales job,

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.710
but like figuring out different
ways that you could go

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:01.360
out of your way to
create a better customer experience.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.090
Like if somebody mentioned
they like needed sunscreen,

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.670
like going to grab that,
and bringing it out to them.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:10.030
And so it was just kind
of making me think about when

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:13.870
you're serving these insights as
well and like figuring out

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.700
the best way to share
that information.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.000
So your end user has the
best experience possible.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:24.160
I always love being able
to tie-in like past roles like

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.830
that, and lessons
learned. But RM,

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:30.140
I saw you had a comment
on that thread, too.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:33.920
So I was curious if you wanted
to add anything on that.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:35.990
RM: Hi, thanks. No,
not a whole lot to add,

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:39.660
but just wanted to say that
sales skills come up in surprising ways.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:43.430
You might have the most
wonderful piece of analysis,

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.440
but you can't convey that
to your stakeholders.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:46.910
That's essentially worthless.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:49.460
So it's important to remember,
no matter how cool your code

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:51.185
is, or how powerful
your analysis is,

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.000
you still have to
sell the thing.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.805
I know the sale sometimes
is downplayed a little bit, but

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:57.635
it's an important
skill to have.

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:00.145
- Absolutely.

00:15:00.145 --> 00:15:01.600
SEAN: Oh, yeah, for sure. I've

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.750
noticed that
firsthand, where yeah,

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:07.550
you can have the fanciest
models or anything like that,

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:09.820
but unless you can surface
it to them in something that's

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:13.660
meaningful to them, it's
exactly at that point,

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:14.590
where you can have--

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:15.550
if it lives--

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:15.880
was it--

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:17.140
I think it was David
Robinson, right?

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.870
If it lives on your
laptop, it's essentially useless, right?

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:23.800
You need to ship it or
send out something so that people can

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.380
actually see it or appreciate
it, right? Because like yeah,

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.820
it's great on your
computer on the code.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:29.100
But

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:32.440
will people actually be
able to use it, right?

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:33.700
It's cool that
you know about it,

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:36.400
but you want to share it
with everyone else, right? So yeah.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:39.550
RACHAEL: Absolutely Alan,
I see you had a question.

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:40.660
You wanted to jump in here?

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:45.130
ALAN: Yeah. Hey,
everybody. Sean,

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:47.680
I'm really curious about
how you figured out--

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.520
as the first data role
in the org, how did you,

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:52.300
and your leaders,

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.730
and your peers figure out
what success would look like for

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:57.340
you? Because on one
hand, you've got this--

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.620
you've got some autonomy.
You can do [? PSCs. ?]

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:01.210
At some point, you need
to figure out like,

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:02.920
does that get formalized into

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:06.160
like, am I
successful at my job?

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.900
And I'm wondering what that
process was like for you.

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:11.480
How did you get
agreement on? Like, OK,

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.650
I think this is what I
do now. And people are

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:16.930
happy with that. What
was that process like?

00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:18.860
How did you figure that
out organizationally?

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:22.570
SEAN: So I think my role has
evolved over time at [? S2G. ?]

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:25.900
And at first, it's just
that we were trying to--

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:29.080
we had all this information, right?
We wanted to be able to--

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.150
because at the end of
the day, technically,

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:32.560
for the bottom
line for [? S2G ?]

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:37.330
is like being able to invest
in innovative companies, and then hopefully,

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:42.330
being able to generate market returns for
the fund at the end of the day.

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.900
And so for us, it
was clear, like, hey,

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.300
are we driving value for
our portfolio companies?

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:49.380
Because if they're
successful, then in the end,

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:53.570
we'll be successful. And so
it was clear for us, is being

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:56.360
able to identify let's say,

00:16:56.360 --> 00:17:00.710
investors that we would want
to co-invest with or other

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:01.550
companies, right?

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:03.620
So it was clear
from there. Like,

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:07.099
if I'm able to identify
investors or companies that are

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:09.920
relevant to us, then therefore,

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.300
it will help the bottom
line of the organization.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:13.229
And so

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:15.730
in terms of how

00:17:16.730 --> 00:17:17.640
I'm successful,

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.780
so if I'm able to
identify or create different models that

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:23.299
allows us to find

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:27.619
out a company that's a diamond
in the rough, or an investor,

00:17:27.619 --> 00:17:30.530
or someone that we may not
have interacted otherwise,

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:32.050
we allow them to

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:35.520
find them by doing analysis,
and things like that.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:39.440
And therefore, that's how at least
for me, I was successful, right?

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:43.400
And so one way to do that is
to be able to of interview the

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:46.490
different stakeholders, not
necessarily your manager per

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:49.770
se, right? You get guidance
from them. But then you interview

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:53.270
stakeholders, identify
their pain points, right?

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:53.840
It's like, oh,

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:55.490
every time--

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:59.150
I'm scrambling right before my meeting
to find a talking points to

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:03.430
whoever I'm meeting with, right? So
I was like, OK, let's take that,

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:06.170
take the different data
sources using the contextual knowledge

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:09.110
of the different data sources
that we have. So for us,

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:12.050
we use Salesforce,
other data sources,

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:15.320
trying to integrate them,
and then provide a proof of

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:16.640
concept, or whatever.

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:20.720
And then you drive value there
because then you're able to say, like,

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:24.230
remember when you went to me
and you said I have trouble

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:27.380
figuring out what I'm going to
talk to about with this person.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:29.270
I'm able to provide
that. Therefore,

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:31.290
I give them something
that they like.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.200
And that's how you can kind
be successful in that regard,

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:35.270
at least for me.

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:37.000
Yeah.

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:42.550
- Just to follow up a
tiny bit, was there anybody who, again,

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:43.990
as the first role of the kind,

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:45.910
was there anybody who thought
you were going to be doing

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:50.140
something different? And you
had to reconcile like, oh,

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:51.640
no, I think it's this--

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:52.000
SEAN: Yeah.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:53.620
ALAN: --and then
figure it out together?

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:56.890
- For sure, yeah. I think
yeah. We had different tools.

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:59.920
And then I think for me, as
I knew what I wanted to do.

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.055
But then sometimes you can't do
that because you need like the data

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:06.430
foundation there, right? You
have duplicate accounts. You have--

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:10.540
there's so much things that you
need to cleanse and to be able

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:13.340
to run the cool, fancy models,
which I would love to do.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.850
But the thing is that sometimes
the data is not there.

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:18.200
It's not clean. So
it's like getting--

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:22.720
I think for me, it's like
you create the PoC, and then you-- because you

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:25.030
know what you need to do
in terms of cleaning the

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:26.650
data, get the data sources,

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:28.480
and actually make it
so that it's usable.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:31.360
So that you can do
it relevant analysis.

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:33.940
So you kind of give them
a nugget that they would like.

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:37.910
And then on the back end,
you kind of fix stuff and

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:41.240
make it cleaner so that
you can actually make more

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:46.510
feature rich PoCs. But yeah,
definitely it's a struggle, right?

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:47.500
A,

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.300
Getting the buy-in to get the
tools to be able to do the

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:50.260
thing that they want,

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:55.150
and then kind of getting
the buy-in from them. But yeah,

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:57.325
it's definitely
a struggle. So--

00:19:58.120 --> 00:19:58.570
ALAN: Thanks.

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:01.150
SEAN: But then you have autonomy.
I had a lot of autonomy.

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:04.150
So that's like the pros and
cons of like being on a big

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:05.700
team versus being

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.680
the solo contributor.

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:09.970
ALAN: Yeah. Thanks.

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:13.150
RACHAEL: I know you mentioned
in one of the answers before,

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:17.440
like trying to advocate for
tools when you already have

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:22.030
something like a BI tool. But
I was wondering, do you also work

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:24.550
alongside with the BI tools?

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:27.800
Or are there any
use cases with that?

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.320
- Yeah. So what
I've done recently--

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:30.820
so

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:36.985
we had some BI tools before. And
but then what we didn't have was--

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:39.270
because sometimes
those tools require

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:42.760
essentially more or
less model data layers,

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:44.470
where it's like pretty clear.

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:45.850
Like when you're
querying from this,

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:49.060
you feel confident in the
data that you're pulling from in

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:50.740
terms of the rows,
and things like that.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:53.070
But

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:58.600
what I had to do is
I had to create ETL pipelines.

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:02.830
So it's just getting data
from our raw data sources,

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:03.820
transforming them,

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:05.830
and modeling them so
that they're actually usable,

00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:09.540
and then that actually feeds
our BI tools downstream.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:12.280
And so at least for me,

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:16.120
we use Posit Connect to
have scripts that take the data,

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:20.140
do the thing, and then send
it back to our data warehouse.

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:23.380
And then from there, our
BI analysts can take the data,

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:26.080
and do whatever they need
to do, and feel confident about

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:28.180
it, rather than pulling
from the raw logs.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:33.170
We're able to just point to
we call it a model data layer,

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.270
right? And so then
you know confidently,

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:37.490
with high confidence
that what you're

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:40.490
displaying is accurate, right?

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:43.220
So it's not like we're trying
to replace one another.

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:44.585
We live in harmony.

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:47.075
So I'm able to take the data,

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:50.480
clean it, and then run my
own analysis. But then other

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:52.160
people, like other not

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:57.410
power users that can use it
to run analysis on themselves and

00:21:57.410 --> 00:22:01.730
not have to worry about is
this correct or not. So, yeah.

00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:02.510
RACHAEL: Thank you.

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:05.510
Frank, I see you had a
question a bit earlier.

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:06.335
Want to jump in here?

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:09.750
- Hey, Sean. Thanks for
all your insights so far.

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.330
You started talking
about modeling.

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:14.360
And you mentioned like building
a model and finding the gems

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.670
that wouldn't be
seen otherwise.

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:19.010
And it got me wondering a
lot of this conversation has been

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:20.660
focused on sales,

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:24.140
or like how you pitch
data science in the value,

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:26.450
and how you get information to
the right people at the right

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:27.385
place at the right time.

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.230
The question popped
into my head.

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:33.170
If you had to ballpark it,

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:35.900
this is not a super
quantitative answer I'm looking

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:36.800
for, but I'm curious,

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:39.575
if you look back,
what would you say

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:42.245
you attribute your success to

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:48.000
like 50% 50%? Is it that
the sales and the operations and how

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:53.080
you delivered data science
to your organization versus

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:56.330
the modeling and the
statistical rigor

00:22:57.320 --> 00:22:59.630
that you do kind of
like behind the scenes?

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:02.710
SEAN: In terms of how--

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:05.140
where do I spend my
time or in terms of

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:07.180
what metrics, sorry?

00:23:07.180 --> 00:23:10.030
FRANK: Yeah. No, like what
do you attribute your success to?

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:10.210
SEAN: Oh.

00:23:10.210 --> 00:23:12.860
- It sounds like you've
been pretty successful in your role.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:14.710
So if you had to look back,
and reflect, and say, like, oh,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:15.580
my success, right,

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:17.890
I do a lot of delivery,

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:20.410
and like selling
to the organization versus--

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:20.920
SEAN: Yeah.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:22.330
- Yeah. The--

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:23.440
SEAN: Yeah. So I would say--

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:24.600
it

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:27.910
was a struggle at
the beginning, right?

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:31.540
Like there's no doubt about
it, of getting all the

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:36.160
data getting the model
and then we like--

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.120
what I did was I was able to--

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:42.730
I would say maybe spent like
I don't know, 75% of the time

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:47.680
getting the value in the sense
that getting the nugget of information.

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:50.290
But it was in like a
colab notebook, right?

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:51.880
So I had this machine
learning pipeline.

00:23:51.880 --> 00:23:54.850
And it was essentially, white
glove service of like, Sean,

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:57.670
give me the top 100
investors for this company, or whatever,

00:23:57.670 --> 00:24:00.560
right? And so I would do
the analysis and run that stuff.

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:01.940
But then they were
like, oh, you know what?

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:03.980
I want someone that's going
to write a $10 million check.

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:06.290
So then I have to go
in and change it, change it, change

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:08.720
it. So then what I ended
up doing was like, OK,

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:11.840
this is not scalable. We
need to productionalize this to a

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.240
PoC, like a
Streamlit application, right?

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:15.800
So it's like a
Python app, web app.

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:17.840
They can kind of do whatever
they want, change the

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:20.450
parameters, do it.
It's self-service.

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:21.770
But

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.050
I couldn't do that until I
had like the foundation of the

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:28.125
model, so to speak. So it's
a ton of time went into that,

00:24:28.125 --> 00:24:29.875
that was not a good use of

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:34.240
time in the sense of tweaking
every parameter for them.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.480
And so then I was like, OK,
let's create a web application.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.630
And then that was a
beast in and of itself.

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:43.920
But then once that's up
and running, you're kind of smooth

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:46.500
sailing from there.
So it's like 75%.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:50.760
And then the other 25% for
me was creating a web application

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:55.800
or some type of widget thing
that the end unit or the

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.690
business unit could use to be
able to do whatever they want.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:01.680
And I call it productionalizing
it because it's like--

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:05.650
it's no longer in a notebook,
but it's an actual app, right?

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:09.270
And then another caveat is
like the whole deploying.

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:11.040
Like when you're in the
enterprise, you can't just--

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:13.020
I would love to just
deploy it in GitHub,

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:14.430
and make it open to the world.

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:17.280
But because it's proprietary
information, you have to--

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:19.110
that's another, like,
a security thing,

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:20.790
getting buy in
from your IT people

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:24.970
because that's a whole beast
