toy example that does one thing.

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:41.440
And it's actually
quite helpful, right?

00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:43.000
Because then it
frees up my time,

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:44.755
makes them a little bit easier.

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:47.665
It's one of those
things that I think--

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:50.910
it doesn't have to be
complex because I think--

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:52.800
even I thought so.

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:55.590
I wanted to have the most
sophisticated app that has all the--

00:37:55.590 --> 00:37:58.030
it's like a Ferrari, right?
But you don't need that.

00:37:58.030 --> 00:38:00.090
You just need something
simple, right?

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:04.020
If it does one thing, and
it does it well and robustly,

00:38:04.020 --> 00:38:07.590
people will begin to use it
over and over and over, right?

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:08.720
And that's--

00:38:09.310 --> 00:38:10.050
it's so funny.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:12.480
It's like I was so proud
of this app that I made,

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:14.790
but it's like the simple
thing, like the pivot table.

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:17.580
It's like people like that,
right? And it's like, whatever.

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:19.965
It's cool. It did not
take me nearly as long.

00:38:20.610 --> 00:38:24.270
But sometimes it's those little
small things that it does it

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:27.660
well. And that's what can get people

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:32.040
really excited about
whatever you're producing.

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:36.240
- I love that. How do you find
out what those things are,

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:39.510
that are taking people way
longer than it should or the

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:41.448
opportunities to help somebody?

00:38:41.448 --> 00:38:42.000
- Yeah.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:43.650
So I think it's just one
of those things where it's like

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:46.410
pattern recognition where
someone has come to you more

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:49.300
than three times to do this
one thing because you do it well.

00:38:49.300 --> 00:38:50.035
It's very simple,

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:55.210
whether it be deduping,
or looking up, or aggregating.

00:38:55.210 --> 00:39:00.250
So for us, we want to aggregate
our quarterly metrics or

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:04.690
KPIs. And so I do that a
couple of times, just like, you know what?

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:08.290
Maybe we should make this
into a little self-service

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:11.350
application, so that they can
kind of do whatever they want.

00:39:11.350 --> 00:39:13.240
They can kind of tweak things,

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:15.205
and then they'll be
able to get out--

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:19.210
the analysis that they need.

00:39:19.210 --> 00:39:20.800
And so it's just kind of that--

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:22.180
it's another David
Robinson thing.

00:39:22.180 --> 00:39:23.680
Don't do it more than
three times, right?

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:25.570
They'll create a function.
But in this case,

00:39:25.570 --> 00:39:29.530
if you do something maybe you
create a Shiny app or whatever

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:34.150
app that can help them do
whatever they need to do more efficiently.

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:35.200
Yeah.

00:39:35.900 --> 00:39:37.880
RACHAEL: Cool.
Thank you. Kevin,

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:40.145
I see your comment
here in the chat

00:39:40.970 --> 00:39:44.060
about having a repository
of these simple apps.

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:47.420
Would you mind just sharing
a little bit more about what

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:52.100
you'd like to see there? Are
you thinking like for example, companies or--

00:39:52.100 --> 00:39:53.390
- Yeah. No, I was just thinking

00:39:55.314 --> 00:39:57.810
that I think that's a
great, like the pivot table thing. I

00:39:57.810 --> 00:39:59.532
think there are these

00:40:00.920 --> 00:40:02.690
things that non-power

00:40:03.470 --> 00:40:03.970
users

00:40:04.610 --> 00:40:08.330
end up having trouble doing.
And across lots of industries,

00:40:08.330 --> 00:40:09.860
it's probably the same.

00:40:09.860 --> 00:40:15.290
There's probably a handful of
tasks that people across all

00:40:15.290 --> 00:40:16.940
industries are doing in Excel

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:19.960
that could be done with

00:40:20.960 --> 00:40:22.790
a nifty web app.

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:23.800
And

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:25.640
yeah, I mean, it might be--

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:28.260
yeah, I was just thinking
it would be cool if

00:40:28.940 --> 00:40:32.300
an organization like Posit,
who has access to all of us, you

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:33.710
know? If somehow--

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:35.330
yeah, I'm not sure how we'd

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:38.490
prioritize them because it
wouldn't be helpful if you have

00:40:38.490 --> 00:40:42.330
a library of like 1,000 web apps
that do like all sorts of crazy things.

00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:47.000
But if somehow it was curated

00:40:48.670 --> 00:40:50.100
really well, then--

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:51.030
like for example,

00:40:51.030 --> 00:40:53.040
this pivot table app that
Sean's talking about,

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:54.720
we could probably all use that.

00:40:55.290 --> 00:40:57.150
And if it's like a
Shiny app or something,

00:40:57.150 --> 00:41:00.840
everyone with a Posit server
could just download it from

00:41:00.840 --> 00:41:02.550
GitHub, and spin it up,

00:41:03.765 --> 00:41:04.560
and try it.

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:06.165
So yeah,

00:41:06.990 --> 00:41:10.320
that was the idea. But
again, it would take some curation,

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:11.145
which would be tricky.

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:13.770
But it could be valuable
if it's done right.

00:41:15.111 --> 00:41:15.560
- Yeah.

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:16.090
RACHAEL: Yeah. I love it.

00:41:17.800 --> 00:41:18.070
SEAN: Yeah.

00:41:18.070 --> 00:41:20.300
- Yeah. I'm learning
from all of you.

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:22.420
And when we're talking about
building business cases,

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:25.300
I need to have these examples,
where now I can say, look.

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:27.940
Kevin from the FDA
asked about this.

00:41:27.940 --> 00:41:28.333
KEVIN: [LAUGHS]

00:41:28.333 --> 00:41:29.470
RACHAEL: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:30.010
go do it.

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:33.280
So but seriously,

00:41:33.280 --> 00:41:37.090
if there are different apps
like this or examples that you

00:41:37.090 --> 00:41:39.130
all are thinking of, or
you're like, oh, I have one.

00:41:39.130 --> 00:41:40.330
I maybe could contribute.

00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:43.700
That would help me in building
a case for something like this.

00:41:43.700 --> 00:41:46.990
So I started a
thread in the chat,

00:41:46.990 --> 00:41:48.730
if anybody wants to
comment in there.

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:52.730
OK. Awesome. Let me jump to

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:56.220
scrolling up some of
the questions. So RM,

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:58.470
I see you asked a question
about working with IT.

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.300
Do you want to jump
in here and ask that?

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:02.600
RM: Sure. Thanks.

00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:05.550
Just following up on the thread
that was mentioned earlier,

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:06.980
I was wondering if you
have any tips or tricks,

00:42:06.980 --> 00:42:09.500
or things that work for you
and talking to a common problem

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:12.230
that I've seen is IT doesn't
really talk data science.

00:42:12.230 --> 00:42:13.940
And data science doesn't
probably talk IT.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:16.880
Do you have to learn
key concepts, like what is a

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:19.490
server? How do you secure
it? Like basic stuff like that?

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:21.050
That you may or may not know?

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:22.655
Or conversely, do
you have to teach

00:42:23.330 --> 00:42:25.910
IT here? This is what
ETL, is what it does.

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:27.590
Here's why data is not clean.

00:42:27.590 --> 00:42:29.420
I'm curious to know
what tips and tricks

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.970
you might suggest or things
that might have worked for you.

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:35.180
- Yeah. I think for me,

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:39.860
understanding whatever
protocol that they use.

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:42.260
At least for us, we--

00:42:42.260 --> 00:42:43.820
remember I'm not like a cloud

00:42:44.570 --> 00:42:45.590
security guy.

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:48.230
Learning that we use Okta,

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:50.360
and making sure that
all the things that we

00:42:52.100 --> 00:42:53.945
had were secured via SSL

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:59.430
when we were spinning up
Posit Connect within our organization,

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:02.460
making sure that we had Okta
authenticate everything,

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:06.960
and making sure that using the
keywords or buzzwords that they identify with.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:10.380
Because to your point, I
don't necessarily speak IT natively,

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:13.350
all the security.
And then conversely,

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:15.390
they don't speak data
science necessarily, right?

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:16.650
But to make sure,
it's like, hey,

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:20.910
I understand this is a high
concern for you. And I share

00:43:20.910 --> 00:43:24.000
that as well. And here's
what we've done to address that.

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.055
Does this sound
reasonable to you?

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:30.570
And so like almost meeting them
where they are is quite helpful,

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.210
even though you may not speak
exactly the same language,

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:36.660
right? So like my understanding
with Okta and all that is

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:40.410
we're using that as the
gatekeeper to authenticate

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:41.530
everything. And once you

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:45.100
use that and you're compliant,
then the IT people were

00:43:45.900 --> 00:43:48.240
relieved, or like,
OK, this is good.

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:51.270
But then I think your mileage
may vary depending on the

00:43:51.270 --> 00:43:52.815
organization. I don't
know how it's like-- like

00:43:52.815 --> 00:43:55.605
with the FDA, right? I
don't know things like that.

00:43:57.270 --> 00:43:58.450
In terms of how

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:02.310
it is. So it is
context dependent.

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:05.880
But in terms of the general
tips that I found that helped

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:08.850
me out was like
communicating, meeting them.

00:44:09.360 --> 00:44:12.000
Even if you know it's secure,

00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:14.520
like making sure that
they understand that you're not

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:16.290
trying to go around them

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:18.780
to do that.

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:21.345
RM: Thank you.
That makes sense.

00:44:21.345 --> 00:44:21.845
SEAN: Mm-hmm.

00:44:23.770 --> 00:44:25.860
- Thank you. I see a
few anonymous questions.

00:44:25.860 --> 00:44:28.320
I want to go over
to as well. And

00:44:29.370 --> 00:44:30.260
one was

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:33.130
it says thanks a lot.

00:44:33.130 --> 00:44:38.440
Can you share how you tackled
data silos and access company data sources?

00:44:38.440 --> 00:44:41.410
Did you handle it alone or
seek support from other teams?

00:44:42.472 --> 00:44:46.390
SEAN: Yeah. So data silos
can exist. I think that's--

00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:49.110
for me,

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:53.100
it was not necessarily data
silos because all of our stuff

00:44:53.100 --> 00:44:54.915
is in the cloud. So
we use Google Cloud--

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:58.590
Google Cloud platform for
our stuff. But for me,

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:02.250
data was siloed and the
brains of the business unit.

00:45:02.250 --> 00:45:06.760
And so what we've done
was do informational interviews where you

00:45:09.060 --> 00:45:13.500
go tag along with them right to see
how do they go about doing their job.

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:17.010
And so by going to
let's say the business development team

00:45:17.010 --> 00:45:19.470
or the investment team,
and kind of shadowing them,

00:45:19.470 --> 00:45:20.820
you understand

00:45:21.680 --> 00:45:23.512
wither their pain
points, or what

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:30.570
you can do as a data scientist
to help make their job easier, right?

00:45:30.570 --> 00:45:35.730
And so that's kind of what
I had to do to understand

00:45:35.730 --> 00:45:38.490
that, oh, they're really
looking for this information.

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:41.850
And it is there. It's just
maybe not easily accessible to

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:43.680
them, right? And so
that's how you do that. OK,

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:47.670
like, at least for me, all the data
is in like BigQuery, and all the--

00:45:47.670 --> 00:45:50.110
because we feed everything
into the data warehouse.

00:45:50.110 --> 00:45:51.960
And then we do all the
transformations and send it

00:45:51.960 --> 00:45:56.880
back to a clean layer, right?
But in order for me to know

00:45:56.880 --> 00:45:59.040
what to extract, because
there's so much raw data,

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.090
you almost have to understand
what is the business trying to

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:04.140
achieve, right?

00:46:04.140 --> 00:46:06.030
Because sometimes I've
done things all the time,

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:11.140
where I do stuff that's what
I think is like addresses what

00:46:11.140 --> 00:46:13.450
they need, but it's not
exactly what they want.

00:46:14.110 --> 00:46:17.740
It's like they may ask for A, B,
C, but they really need D, E, F, right?

00:46:18.370 --> 00:46:21.310
But you don't realize that
until you talk to them.

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:24.550
Even though they say I want A,
B, C, they really need D, E, F.

00:46:24.550 --> 00:46:28.315
And you don't get that
until you're actually kind understand things

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:31.930
because we are working
with everything. But

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:36.320
that's what I've noticed, is
you deliver what they ask for,

00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:39.040
but not necessarily what they
need. If that makes sense.

00:46:39.040 --> 00:46:42.640
So it's like you only get
that by kind of iterating,

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:46.900
understanding where data may
be or whatever problem that

00:46:46.900 --> 00:46:49.150
they're trying to solve
in terms of a silo.

00:46:49.150 --> 00:46:53.080
And then you'll be able to
integrate it that way. But I

00:46:53.080 --> 00:46:55.640
realize that may not be
the case because some

00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:58.870
enterprises have
true data silos,

00:46:58.870 --> 00:47:00.250
where it's like a
totally different.

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:02.740
Server 1 is on prem,
one is in the cloud,

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:05.980
one you don't have access
rights to, if it's health care

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:10.870
data. So I can't speak to that. But
I can at least speak to my experience.

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:13.040
RACHAEL:

00:47:13.040 --> 00:47:16.430
How do you figure out more
quickly what they actually need

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:19.910
if it's not what they
told you they wanted?

00:47:19.910 --> 00:47:20.410
SEAN: Yeah.

00:47:21.470 --> 00:47:23.600
Because I was in grad school,

00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:27.110
I'm used to working late night
hours and just working on it

00:47:27.110 --> 00:47:28.980
for just brute force
figuring it out.

00:47:28.980 --> 00:47:32.480
But what I had to realize
when I pivoted the industry is being

00:47:32.480 --> 00:47:36.680
able to iterate quickly, to be
able to show them like a janky

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:38.490
looking report or
with some number.

00:47:39.140 --> 00:47:41.420
It's like, is this
right? Is this not right?

00:47:41.420 --> 00:47:43.070
Is this in the ballpark?

00:47:43.070 --> 00:47:48.190
And then getting feedback or
directionality because I do like that,

00:47:49.430 --> 00:47:53.300
like the Shiny well
polished application. But it's like

00:47:53.300 --> 00:47:55.190
it'll get to that thing
where you work on it,

00:47:55.190 --> 00:47:58.550
but then it's like they're like
this is not what I wanted, right?

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:01.400
And so you're much better
off showing something that's really

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:04.890
rough, but it's somewhat
tangentially relevant to them.

00:48:04.890 --> 00:48:08.060
And then iterating again,
then to take all the time,

00:48:08.060 --> 00:48:09.890
make it super
beautiful looking,

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:13.815
but it's like not the values
or the information that they want, right?

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:17.670
So that was kind of one thing
I had to kind of unlearn when I

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:19.980
was in graduate school
to the industry is like

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:25.730
to make sure you're
getting feedback from them. Yeah.

00:48:26.990 --> 00:48:28.250
RACHAEL: That's
great. Thank you.

00:48:28.250 --> 00:48:28.750
SEAN: Mm-hmm.

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:33.770
- Alan, I think you have
a question in the chat,

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:36.080
also a comment. So I'll
just have you jump in.

00:48:36.080 --> 00:48:37.850
I wasn't reading it all yet.

00:48:37.850 --> 00:48:39.440
- Oh, no. I don't
want to be the--

00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:41.420
my question is more
of a comment guy.

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:43.580
- No, no, not at all. I just--

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:45.440
can't use both
parts of my brain.

00:48:46.310 --> 00:48:49.040
- I think I have two
competing things going on in my brain.

00:48:49.040 --> 00:48:52.910
One of which is I love the
idea of the pivot table use case and

00:48:52.910 --> 00:48:55.790
all it opens up of
making nimble flexible,

00:48:56.510 --> 00:48:59.220
ad hoc tools that really
meet a business solution.

00:48:59.220 --> 00:49:00.680
And I love that
because it's elegant.

00:49:00.680 --> 00:49:03.140
And it's exciting way to work.

00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:04.040
And at the same time,

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:08.570
I think it competes with
push back about proliferation of

00:49:08.570 --> 00:49:09.860
solutions and

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:15.170
a different avenue of doing
work, which would be like

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:18.290
saying, hey, the analyst has just
got to get better at RBI,

00:49:18.290 --> 00:49:20.150
or whatever, right?

00:49:20.150 --> 00:49:24.340
And so I'm really curious about what
are the stories to tell that motivate

00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:28.560
the kind of work that we all
really, really super love

00:49:28.560 --> 00:49:31.020
versus that other story.

00:49:31.750 --> 00:49:33.240
And so I'm curious if--

00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:33.540
Sean,

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:37.470
if you or if others
have that success story that motivates

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:39.555
that way to work,
as opposed to the-- like,

00:49:39.555 --> 00:49:40.230
well, no,

00:49:40.230 --> 00:49:43.290
you use the Enterprise Vi
app channel way to work.

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:48.765
Because I think those
are in somewhat tension. Yeah.

00:49:49.830 --> 00:49:51.400
- I totally agree. I think

00:49:52.710 --> 00:49:55.800
it is for us because
we're a little bit smaller, right?

00:49:55.800 --> 00:49:58.350
We don't have that problem yet.

00:49:58.350 --> 00:50:01.830
But I can definitely envision
this being something that is if

00:50:01.830 --> 00:50:04.930
you have a larger team,
where yeah, to your point,

00:50:04.930 --> 00:50:07.980
the analysts or whoever
should get more proficient.

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:08.480
But

00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:11.260
at least in my instances, it's

00:50:12.060 --> 00:50:13.560
our admin that needs--

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:16.620
that just wants a quick
calculation to get on to do

00:50:16.620 --> 00:50:18.040
whatever they need to do.

00:50:18.040 --> 00:50:21.060
But I think that's one of
those things where at least for me,

00:50:21.060 --> 00:50:23.160
if it's in Connect, you
can look at the usage

00:50:24.000 --> 00:50:26.820
metrics, and theoretically, look at
the logs to see like,

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:28.080
oh, maybe this person--

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:31.690
And I think you have a
conversation. It's like, OK,

00:50:31.690 --> 00:50:35.340
it seems this is
really valuable to you.

00:50:35.340 --> 00:50:36.690
And then you can
kind of address,

00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:38.460
like maybe they
need to get better.

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:40.900
But I think you have to
be strategic about that.

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:43.650
You don't want to
be like logging people's activity.

00:50:43.650 --> 00:50:44.990
But I think another

00:50:45.870 --> 00:50:49.850
instance is let's say you have
a suite of really small toy

00:50:50.550 --> 00:50:53.715
shiny apps, or whatever for pivot
tables and all that, you can almost

00:50:54.360 --> 00:50:58.740
making that a featured
application that allows them to

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:00.960
do whatever they need to
do. So let's [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:00.960 --> 00:51:03.150
they need to pivot. They need
to crop. And they need to,

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:05.790
I don't know, make sure that
the data is not missing.

00:51:06.330 --> 00:51:09.300
You can almost package that
into a larger application that

00:51:09.300 --> 00:51:10.455
allows them to do that.

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:15.160
But I do I do see your
point about like--

00:51:15.160 --> 00:51:17.910
there is a time and a
place to use the Enterprise preferred

00:51:18.990 --> 00:51:21.045
business intelligence
tools, right?

00:51:22.860 --> 00:51:24.660
And that's tricky, for sure.

00:51:24.660 --> 00:51:25.060
ALAN: Yeah.

00:51:25.060 --> 00:51:25.350
SEAN: Yeah

00:51:25.350 --> 00:51:26.250
- For sure.

00:51:26.250 --> 00:51:26.750
SEAN: Yeah.

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:28.350
- Thanks.

00:51:28.350 --> 00:51:28.557
-

00:51:28.557 --> 00:51:32.720
One anonymous question was can
you share how you developed the

00:51:32.720 --> 00:51:37.670
intuition as a data scientist
or statistician to make certain decisions?

00:51:39.650 --> 00:51:41.570
- Intuition, yeah, I
think for you [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:41.570 --> 00:51:45.410
for me, you get like that
code sensor, like that--

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:50.270
you get the inkling for me
what the business would want, right?

00:51:50.270 --> 00:51:53.100
And then from there, it's
kind of testing the water.

00:51:53.100 --> 00:51:56.690
So what I do is I
have folks that aren't necessarily the

00:51:56.690 --> 00:51:58.010
super senior managers,

00:51:58.010 --> 00:52:00.455
but I have the junior level
people that are in the--

00:52:01.190 --> 00:52:03.240
working on the business.

00:52:03.240 --> 00:52:06.530
And I will send them little
snippets of projects,

00:52:08.060 --> 00:52:08.870
works in progress.

00:52:09.500 --> 00:52:13.400
And then I almost use them
as a barometer in the right

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:17.150
direction. And then if it
is, then we kind of go there.

00:52:17.150 --> 00:52:18.740
So that's like when
I did the [? S2G ?]

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:19.240
wrapped.

00:52:19.700 --> 00:52:21.810
I went to a couple of
our associates. And I was like,

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:22.790
hey, what do you
guys think of this?

00:52:22.790 --> 00:52:23.960
Does this look and feel right?

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:24.460
Because

00:52:25.310 --> 00:52:27.440
sometimes when you're
presenting to management,

00:52:27.440 --> 00:52:31.220
if the values are off, they're going to
like, why is this off? Why is this--

00:52:31.220 --> 00:52:31.430
right?

00:52:31.430 --> 00:52:33.890
And so you go to the
people that are a little bit more

00:52:33.890 --> 00:52:37.080
junior and you ask them like,
hey, does this feel right?

00:52:37.080 --> 00:52:38.970
Does this seem
right? Does it not?

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:43.170
And what would you want on
the next slide or whatever, right?

00:52:43.170 --> 00:52:47.025
And so then you kind of use
them to be able to guide yourself

00:52:48.420 --> 00:52:49.210
on producing

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:54.220
different applications or
products, what data products.

00:52:54.220 --> 00:52:54.840
- Thank you.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:58.890
Kevin, I realize I missed a
question from you earlier about

00:53:00.030 --> 00:53:03.030
getting feedback from stakeholders. Do
you want to add anything

00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:04.680
there or was--

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:07.380
KEVIN: Yeah. Sure.
Yeah, I think it's--

00:53:08.400 --> 00:53:09.100
my question really-- what I

00:53:09.100 --> 00:53:11.850
wanted to ask like how do you
manage like the kind of

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:14.490
long term vision, right,

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:19.380
when maybe the management
doesn't really know what's possible.

00:53:19.380 --> 00:53:21.390
And they--

00:53:21.390 --> 00:53:21.890
Yeah,

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:24.780
I find myself in this
position sometimes,

00:53:24.780 --> 00:53:27.210
where I'm trying to
figure out how to--

00:53:27.210 --> 00:53:31.330
where long term, we want
to prioritize and build things.

00:53:31.330 --> 00:53:34.003
But the management doesn't
really know what's possible.

00:53:34.810 --> 00:53:36.780
And so it's hard
to get from them,

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:38.500
like what would
they prioritize?

00:53:39.150 --> 00:53:40.130
Like yeah,

00:53:40.130 --> 00:53:43.000
how do you kind of get
priorities from management

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:45.230
that's not in touch with

00:53:46.030 --> 00:53:47.455
what's possible? If
that makes sense.

00:53:47.980 --> 00:53:51.170
- No, for sure. I
think what I've done is

00:53:51.850 --> 00:53:53.095
I do whatever

00:53:55.420 --> 00:54:00.430
is asked of me. So let's
say, it's like the XYZ KPIs, you

00:54:00.430 --> 00:54:02.950
do that. But then sometimes
I'll work on a side project.

00:54:02.950 --> 00:54:03.580
At least for me,

00:54:03.580 --> 00:54:06.100
I was doing like a relational
graph network this year for our

00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:07.870
wrap. And I kind
of slipped that in,

00:54:07.870 --> 00:54:10.120
as like a little thing was
like, I think this is pretty

00:54:10.120 --> 00:54:11.740
cool. They're going to
think it's relevant.

00:54:11.740 --> 00:54:14.630
And you do everything. You're
not going rogue, per se.

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:17.050
But you're just adding
that little, oh,

00:54:17.050 --> 00:54:19.510
look at this cool thing.
And then you kind of--

00:54:19.510 --> 00:54:21.130
and you try to find the most

00:54:22.090 --> 00:54:26.170
not shiny, but more like
interesting thing if you can,

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:29.980
and insert it. And then
that will pique their interest,

00:54:29.980 --> 00:54:31.330
right? And then they'll
ask you for more.

00:54:31.330 --> 00:54:33.730
And then you almost kind
of get that permission to--

00:54:33.730 --> 00:54:37.540
oh, that was just something
I spun up last afternoon.

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:39.160
And then they're like,
oh, yeah, let's do that.

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:41.600
So it's kind of like
the indirect way of doing it.

00:54:41.600 --> 00:54:44.660
And I'm not advocating for
like disobeying your manager,

00:54:44.660 --> 00:54:46.475
or anything like that. But
like what I'm saying is

00:54:48.236 --> 00:54:50.240
they may not know
what's possible.

00:54:50.240 --> 00:54:52.560
And so what we're trying to
do is show them like, hey,

00:54:52.560 --> 00:54:53.930
this might be possible.

00:54:53.930 --> 00:54:56.060
Would you like me to spend
more time on this, right?

00:54:56.060 --> 00:54:57.710
And you still do
whatever you need to do.

00:54:57.710 --> 00:55:02.000
That's the beauty of having
code is you're able to use

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:04.400
something, and recycle
it, and improve it.

00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:05.720
But then at the same time,

00:55:05.720 --> 00:55:08.180
you can kind of work on
other things, and maybe slip that in,

00:55:08.180 --> 00:55:11.250
and then get them to buy in.

00:55:11.250 --> 00:55:11.930
And so that's like--

00:55:11.930 --> 00:55:15.090
truthfully, that's basically the
journey that I've been

00:55:15.950 --> 00:55:18.290
going on, is like I knew
what I wanted to do,

00:55:18.290 --> 00:55:19.730
but like there are certain
things that needed to be in

00:55:19.730 --> 00:55:21.200
place in order to do that.

00:55:21.200 --> 00:55:22.670
So if I wanted to
do a graph network,

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:25.310
I got to make sure the
data is correct, right?

00:55:25.310 --> 00:55:26.030
- Yeah.

00:55:26.030 --> 00:55:29.240
SEAN: Get some way to surface
it to them, and then do that.

00:55:29.240 --> 00:55:30.740
So then you do those things,

00:55:30.740 --> 00:55:33.920
and then you kind of slip
it in once all those prerequisites

00:55:33.920 --> 00:55:35.260
are met. But--

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:35.693
KEVIN: Yeah.

00:55:35.693 --> 00:55:36.560
- [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:36.560 --> 00:55:38.360
KEVIN: No. And my
question is less about--

00:55:38.360 --> 00:55:39.080
yeah,

00:55:39.080 --> 00:55:41.280
giving yourself permission to do
that because I totally get

00:55:41.280 --> 00:55:44.490
that. Yeah, you got to do
it, and show them, right?

00:55:44.490 --> 00:55:47.130
But it's more like just
really big picture, like

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:52.350
how do you get input on
the vision casting for that when--

00:55:52.350 --> 00:55:54.040
you know, because obviously,

00:55:54.040 --> 00:55:56.730
you can make your own vision
of what you think this graph

00:55:56.730 --> 00:55:58.380
thing. You have a
whole vision for it.

00:55:58.380 --> 00:55:59.620
And you can go
forward with that.

00:55:59.620 --> 00:56:01.920
And that's awesome.
But ideally,

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:03.150
when you're working
for an organization,

00:56:03.150 --> 00:56:08.670
you do want to somehow get
input or some direction from the leadership,

00:56:09.270 --> 00:56:11.055
and not just always
be steering them.

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:12.390
- Yeah. No--

00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:12.900
KEVIN: Does that make sense?

00:56:13.680 --> 00:56:14.130
- Yeah.

00:56:14.130 --> 00:56:16.530
I think it's one of those
things where you try to have

00:56:16.530 --> 00:56:19.410
that open conversation
with the management,

00:56:19.410 --> 00:56:22.920
and where you're
getting, like, oh,

00:56:22.920 --> 00:56:27.150
what do you think about next
year us creating an [? S2G ?]

00:56:27.150 --> 00:56:30.840
chat bot, or we're using a
long chain LLMs, type stuff.

00:56:30.840 --> 00:56:34.140
And they may not necessarily
understand, be like, oh,

00:56:34.140 --> 00:56:36.090
a ChatGPT for the
organization, right?

00:56:36.090 --> 00:56:39.040
And so then they're like, oh,
yes. We would love that. Right?

00:56:39.040 --> 00:56:41.040
And it's almost kind of like it

00:56:41.920 --> 00:56:44.680
depends on your relationship with
the management as well. Like

00:56:44.680 --> 00:56:44.965
KEVIN: Yeah.

00:56:44.965 --> 00:56:45.250
SEAN: [INAUDIBLE]

00:56:45.250 --> 00:56:46.390
- No that's really
helpful. Thanks, man.

00:56:46.390 --> 00:56:47.980
SEAN: Having a
conversation would be like,

00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:50.290
let's just brainstorm
what we call--

00:56:50.290 --> 00:56:50.790
at

00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:54.070
least my relationship with the
management is like that where

00:56:54.070 --> 00:56:55.250
you can kind of hey,

00:56:55.250 --> 00:56:57.190
what do you think about
a ChatGPT for the org?

00:56:58.780 --> 00:57:01.030
It's going to be a huge
effort. Don't get me wrong.

00:57:01.030 --> 00:57:02.530
But like you're
having a conversation.

00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:05.440
It's like, is this something
worth pursuing? Is this not?

00:57:06.520 --> 00:57:07.690
And then from there,

00:57:07.690 --> 00:57:10.750
you kind understand
their priorities as well.

00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:13.000
But I acknowledge that it's--

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:15.920
that may not be the case
for some people. I would imagine

00:57:15.920 --> 00:57:18.850
FDA, you can't just waltz up
to the administrator, right?

00:57:18.850 --> 00:57:19.945
But you know,

00:57:21.220 --> 00:57:23.125
trying to understand
and meet with

00:57:23.810 --> 00:57:26.490
the decision
makers, and kind ask

00:57:27.370 --> 00:57:30.040
them about the vision,
and slip some, you know,

00:57:30.040 --> 00:57:33.580
would something like this be useful
or not, is like what I

00:57:34.240 --> 00:57:35.590
was thinking. I don't know.

00:57:35.590 --> 00:57:37.390
KEVIN: Yeah. I think the
brainstorming approach makes

00:57:37.390 --> 00:57:39.350
sense. Yeah, that's
good. Thanks, man.

